= A New Nation Learns to Vote

On 15 August 1947 India became independent — but independence arrived bleeding. Partition had split Bengal and Punjab, killed somewhere near a million people and forced over a million refugees across new borders. The new government had to feed people, resettle families, absorb princely states AND design a political system. The writers of the Constitution then made a choice that still amazes historians: they gave the vote to *every adult*, on day one.

- *Universal adult franchise — the boldest decision:*
  - *The rule (Article 326):* every man and woman aged 21 or above could vote — no property test, no literacy test, no gender bar, no religious bar.
  - *The context makes it huge:* India's literacy in 1951 was barely 18%. Most new voters had never seen a ballot, many had never held a pen.
  - *The contrast:* many older democracies had spent decades, even centuries, extending the vote to the poor and to women. India gave everyone the vote BEFORE it gave everyone schooling — the reverse of the usual path.
  - *The principle:* one person, one vote, one value — the poorest labourer's vote weighed exactly as much as a Maharaja's.

- *The machinery had to be built from zero:*
  - *Election Commission of India:* set up on 25 January 1950 as an independent constitutional authority (Article 324) — free from government control so no ruling party could tilt the field.
  - *Sukumar Sen:* the first Chief Election Commissioner, who ran the impossible logistics of 1951-52.
  - *The scale:* more than *17 crore* eligible voters choosing *489 Members of the Lok Sabha* and about *3,200 Members of Legislative Assemblies* — the two elections held TOGETHER in one giant exercise.
  - *The obstacle:* roughly *85% of voters could not read or write* — a written ballot would have shut them out.
  - *The fix — symbols:* every party got a symbol (the Congress's pair of bullocks, the CPI's ears of corn and sickle...) and candidates were printed with their symbols on the ballot. A voter who could not read a name could still recognise a picture. It made universal franchise actually workable.
  - *The patience it took:* polling ran in phases from October 1951 to February 1952; counting took even longer. Officials trekked to villages with ballot boxes — in some places polling booths were set up within kilometres of every settlement.

#analogy-box(title: "A School That Starts With the Exam")[
  Think of India in 1951 as a school that decided to hold final exams on DAY ONE — before teaching a single class. Most countries teach democracy for generations, then slowly let citizens vote. India let every citizen vote first, trusting elections themselves to teach democracy. The symbols system was the "open book": you did not need to read the whole paper, you only needed to recognise the picture of the party you trusted.
]

= The Three Elections and Their Verdicts

The first three general elections built the rhythm of Indian democracy. Watch the same pattern three times — a giant Congress win — and one big exception where the pattern cracked.

