  [Second, 1957], [371 of 494], [Dominance was not a one-off — it repeated], [CPI 27 — and it WINS Kerala; PSP 19; Jana Sangh 4],
  [Third, 1962], [361 of 494], [Dominance dips but holds; the opposition bench widens], [CPI 29; Swatantra 18; Jana Sangh 14; PSP 12],
)

- *First general election (October 1951 - February 1952):*
  - *Congress 364 of 489 seats* with about 45% of the votes — Nehru became the first elected Prime Minister of free India.
  - *The vote-share puzzle (a favourite question):* 45% of votes produced 74% of seats. Under the *first-past-the-post* system the winner in each constituency takes the seat with a simple lead, so a big nationwide vote lead converts into a far bigger seat lead.
  - *Second largest party:* the *Communist Party of India (16 seats)* — not any socialist party.
  - *Nehru's own margin:* he won from Allahabad (and Phulpur) by lakhs of votes — his campaigning pulled whole states toward Congress.
- *Second general election (1957):*
  - *Congress 371 of 494* — the dominance repeated. Nehru continued as Prime Minister.
  - *The shock headline:* in *Kerala*, the *Communist Party of India won the state assembly* and *E.M.S. Namboodiripad* became Chief Minister — the *first government anywhere in the world to come to power through open, democratic elections on a communist programme*.
  - *The EMS ministry's agenda:* the Land Reforms Act (ending tenancy exploitation) and an Education Bill (regulating private schools) — landowners and religious bodies fought back hard.
  - *1959 — the dismissal:* after violent protests (the "liberation struggle" backed by opposition parties and religious groups), the Centre *asked the Governor for a report and imposed President's rule* — the first time an ELECTED state government was dismissed this way. Kerala became a permanent exam question: 1957 victory, 1959 dismissal.
- *Third general election (1962):*
  - *Congress 361 of 494* — about 20 seats fewer than 1957, but still a massive majority.
  - *The opposition bench matured:* CPI 29, Swatantra 18 (first election after its 1959 founding), Jana Sangh 14, PSP 12 — four organised blocs instead of scattered individuals.
  - *The era's rule:* Congress dominance lasted unbroken from 1952 to *1967* — the year the pattern finally broke (a later chapter).

